  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/6dd511da4a6bab6e37383620852446da06e84207517efc23165544f823ed330f.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $2$ units to the right to give $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/4d60dc4fe52cd717370ae1eeb28dd35b0c3c8e4a4e69ff088b16e56c03be5735.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-2}.$ The point $(2,1)$ moves to $(2,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/e4b042273bfb3a6640e57c75eff3058dd177bc2d75dfdf312160a4a6c9a9de53.png)",
